## DESCRIPTION
## How many boxes should be shaded to shade a fraction of a map.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Arithmetic)
## DBchapter(Fractions/rational numbers)
## DBsection(Interpreting fractions)
## KEYWORDS('vermont', 'map', 'fraction', 'shade')
## Level(5)
## MO(1)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$plot = Plot(xmin => -0.5, xmax => 6.5, ymin => -0.5, ymax => 7.5);
$plot->add_dataset(
    [ 0, 0 ], [ 2, 0 ], [ 2, 3 ], [ 3, 3 ], [ 3, 5 ], [ 4, 5 ],
    [ 4, 7 ], [ 0, 7 ], [ 0, 0 ]
);
$plot->add_dataset(
    [ [ 1, 0 ], [ 1, 7 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 2, 3 ], [ 2, 7 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 3, 5 ], [ 3, 7 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 0, 6 ], [ 4, 6 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 0, 5 ], [ 3, 5 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 0, 4 ], [ 3, 4 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 0, 3 ], [ 2, 3 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 0, 2 ], [ 2, 2 ], width => 1 ],
    [ [ 0, 1 ], [ 2, 1 ], width => 1 ]
);

BEGIN_PGML
Using grid paper, Fletcher made this model of the shape of Vermont.
  

>>[!!]{$plot}{400}<<

He learned that [`\frac{3}{4}`] of Vermont is forest.  Fletcher should  shade [_]{15} boxes gray to represent the fraction of Vermont that is forest.

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
There are 20 boxes in the map of Vermont.  [`\frac{3}{4}\times 20=15`] so he should shade 15 boxes gray
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();


